WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.847 --> 00:00:05.503
One Scrum team has ten people at most; a new packaging machine needs more.

2
00:00:06.545 --> 00:00:11.607
The PLC controls, the screen, the camera inspection and the ERP link.

3
00:00:12.622 --> 00:00:16.999
Six teams do Scrum well and still stall: team A waits for team B.

4
00:00:17.719 --> 00:00:18.683
A dependency.

5
00:00:19.694 --> 00:00:23.177
Scaling: agreements that let many teams deliver as one.

6
00:00:24.200 --> 00:00:30.853
The best known: SAFe, from 2011; version six dates from early 2023.

7
00:00:31.849 --> 00:00:35.378
An annual survey has named SAFe the most used for years.

8
00:00:36.124 --> 00:00:37.436
Note: not a sample.

9
00:00:38.761 --> 00:00:43.441
The heart of SAFe: the Agile Release Train, a team of agile teams.

10
00:00:44.479 --> 00:00:48.275
Usually fifty to a hundred and twenty-five people, long-lived.

11
00:00:49.299 --> 00:00:53.351
The train leaves at fixed times; what is not ready takes the next one.

12
00:00:54.435 --> 00:00:58.847
That rhythm is the cadence: all teams plan, build and deliver at once.

13
00:00:59.829 --> 00:01:04.438
The big cadence is the Planning Interval: the PI lasts eight to twelve weeks.

14
00:01:05.473 --> 00:01:09.572
Iterations last two weeks; the last, the IP iteration,

15
00:01:09.734 --> 00:01:11.394
is for improving and planning.

16
00:01:12.407 --> 00:01:16.784
Like takt on a line: integrate and test together every two weeks.

17
00:01:17.793 --> 00:01:20.533
Ask: which teams deliver something that works together?

18
00:01:21.336 --> 00:01:22.323
That is your train.

19
00:01:23.656 --> 00:01:28.172
Example: a machine builder, seventy-two people on a new line's software,

20
00:01:28.370 --> 00:01:29.426
in teams of nine.

21
00:01:30.452 --> 00:01:35.642
That makes eight teams: within fifty to a hundred and twenty-five, so one ART.

22
00:01:36.659 --> 00:01:42.185
A ten-week PI has five two-week iterations: four to build, one IP iteration.

23
00:01:43.198 --> 00:01:47.842
Per year five point two: about five times all eight teams plan together.

24
00:01:49.167 --> 00:01:55.169
PI Planning aligns the whole train: usually two days, one room, in the IP iteration.

25
00:01:56.178 --> 00:01:58.825
Day one: the business context and the vision.

26
00:01:59.547 --> 00:02:01.613
What must the product do, and why?

27
00:02:02.614 --> 00:02:06.806
The teams plan themselves, per iteration, with their own capacity.

28
00:02:07.840 --> 00:02:12.368
At the end of day one, management looks where capacity or priorities clash.

29
00:02:13.070 --> 00:02:14.951
Day two: adjust and present.

30
00:02:16.031 --> 00:02:20.733
Each team writes PI objectives: short goals in business language.

31
00:02:21.753 --> 00:02:22.380
In doubt?

32
00:02:23.187 --> 00:02:26.194
Then uncommitted: it counts, but is no promise.

33
00:02:27.189 --> 00:02:31.972
The Business Owners, business-accountable, give each goal a value from one to ten.

34
00:02:32.968 --> 00:02:36.195
When time is tight, value nine goes before three.

35
00:02:37.476 --> 00:02:43.096
Dependencies go on the ART Planning Board: teams in rows, iterations in columns.

36
00:02:44.146 --> 00:02:48.686
A red string joins team A's work to team B, which waits for it.

37
00:02:49.760 --> 00:02:54.067
Risks go through ROAM: resolved, owned, accepted or mitigated.

38
00:02:55.107 --> 00:02:59.832
Finally the confidence vote: one to five fingers for how feasible the plan is.

39
00:03:00.863 --> 00:03:05.588
A training rule: an average of three or more is enough, lower means plan again.

40
00:03:06.293 --> 00:03:08.766
Whoever shows one or two gets the floor.

41
00:03:09.785 --> 00:03:11.898
An obeya, Japanese for big room.

42
00:03:12.586 --> 00:03:16.533
The pitfall: the board is full and nobody says it does not fit.

43
00:03:17.914 --> 00:03:22.082
Example: a team has four iterations of thirty story points.

44
00:03:22.795 --> 00:03:24.258
A hundred and twenty.

45
00:03:25.221 --> 00:03:30.480
Twenty percent stays free for unplanned work and breakdowns: ninety-six points.

46
00:03:31.483 --> 00:03:35.176
Report a stoppage with one tap: forty points, value nine.

47
00:03:35.864 --> 00:03:38.767
Report per shift: thirty-five, value six.

48
00:03:39.809 --> 00:03:43.164
Link with the new MES: thirty, value seven.

49
00:03:43.867 --> 00:03:46.375
Together a hundred and five: no fit.

50
00:03:47.436 --> 00:03:48.922
The link goes uncommitted.

51
00:03:49.662 --> 00:03:53.029
Committed: seventy-five points, with room for overrun.

52
00:03:54.323 --> 00:03:59.687
The Release Train Engineer, the RTE, is a servant leader and coach of the train.

53
00:04:00.696 --> 00:04:05.132
Facilitates the events, removes impediments and steers on flow.

54
00:04:06.168 --> 00:04:11.149
No boss handing out tasks; often a former programme manager, a known pitfall.

55
00:04:12.149 --> 00:04:17.223
Product Management decides which solutions are desirable, feasible and viable.

56
00:04:18.246 --> 00:04:21.648
They order the ART Backlog: the features of the whole train.

57
00:04:22.642 --> 00:04:26.264
A feature fits in one PI and has value for a user.

58
00:04:27.291 --> 00:04:31.912
The Product Owner sits with one team and turns features into user stories.

59
00:04:33.218 --> 00:04:38.048
Product Management looks at market and customer, the PO at team and details.

60
00:04:39.056 --> 00:04:42.887
The System Architect guards technical coherence: architecture,

61
00:04:43.073 --> 00:04:44.652
interfaces, groundwork.

62
00:04:45.725 --> 00:04:49.708
Each team has a Scrum Master, in SAFe six also Team Coach.

63
00:04:50.451 --> 00:04:52.715
The Business Owners steer the business side.

64
00:04:53.780 --> 00:04:59.051
Watch names: a product manager at a machine builder often does marketing and sales:

65
00:04:59.225 --> 00:05:00.178
no SAFe role.

66
00:05:01.188 --> 00:05:03.475
A strong PO has mandate and time.

67
00:05:04.184 --> 00:05:05.113
One day a week?

68
00:05:05.931 --> 00:05:07.162
Then teams guess.

69
00:05:08.170 --> 00:05:13.267
Ask: if team three and Product Management disagree, who decides, and how fast?

70
00:05:14.640 --> 00:05:18.158
Example: a maker of weighing systems, six teams.

71
00:05:18.895 --> 00:05:20.114
You watch for a week.

72
00:05:21.151 --> 00:05:26.886
The RTE, a former project manager, hands out tasks on Monday and checks Friday.

73
00:05:27.869 --> 00:05:31.619
Product Management: one person with a sales job besides;

74
00:05:31.805 --> 00:05:34.127
the ART Backlog nine months old.

75
00:05:35.193 --> 00:05:38.595
Three teams share one PO, two afternoons a week.

76
00:05:39.316 --> 00:05:41.139
Which roles are wrong or too thin?

77
00:05:42.118 --> 00:05:44.138
The RTE steers on tasks,

78
00:05:44.185 --> 00:05:49.270
so teams do not learn to plan; Product Management has no time; the PO is too thin.

79
00:05:50.269 --> 00:05:53.879
Proposal: coach the RTE, free up Product Management,

80
00:05:54.100 --> 00:05:56.468
and give each team a PO with mandate.
